## 2. 理由（Rationale）
Explain why paired controlled comparison is the right design:
- Alignment criterion = decision function that maps (generation, gold) to aligned/not-aligned. The claim is about which criterion is better, so we must isolate the criterion as the only variable.
- Same generation outputs → paired design → only the scorer differs → any difference in accuracy is attributable to the criterion.
- Why per-task accuracy + macro-average: consistent with BBH protocol (BBH reports macro-average across tasks, per-task pass rates).
- Why include free-gen tasks: EM brittle on open-ended free text (e.g., "42." vs "42", "B" vs "b"), MinED distance captures near-correctness; this is where the two criteria most plausibly diverge.
- Why threshold sensitivity: the definition of MinED involves a threshold assumption; we must show the conclusion is robust to the threshold choice; otherwise claim is underdetermined.
- Why significance: avoid claiming "outperforms" from noise; paired stats on small task counts.

